WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.276
This video covers the standard USB classes that make a conference bar work on any laptop without a driver install: video, audio, buttons and serial.

00:00:09.676 --> 00:00:14.297
Driver: Kernel code that knows how to operate one kind of hardware. Like: A translator who speaks one specific machine's language.

00:00:15.347 --> 00:00:19.701
The same bar works on Windows, Mac and Linux without installing anything.

00:00:19.951 --> 00:00:25.268
That's not luck. The bar speaks languages every operating system already understands.

00:00:25.518 --> 00:00:29.083
Same bar, same firmware, three different laptops.

00:00:29.333 --> 00:00:36.008
Firmware: Software that runs close to hardware, usually stored in flash memory on the device. Like: The instincts built into an animal: low-level behaviour that is there from birth.

00:00:36.258 --> 00:00:39.544
Let's meet those standard languages, called classes.

00:00:40.594 --> 00:00:43.624
USB classes are standard job descriptions.

00:00:44.024 --> 00:00:50.282
If a device says: I'm a U V C camera, every operating system already has the handbook for that job.

00:00:50.682 --> 00:01:03.557
UVC: USB Video Class: the standard "I am a webcam" language every computer already understands. Like: A universal power plug for cameras: plug in anywhere and it just works, no driver install.

00:01:03.857 --> 00:01:06.226
So no custom driver install is needed.

00:01:06.626 --> 00:01:13.592
And a composite device is one person holding several jobs at once: camera, microphone, speaker and buttons.

00:01:13.992 --> 00:01:20.807
Composite device: One USB device that offers several functions at once, like camera plus mic plus buttons. Like: A multi-tool: one handle, several blades.

00:01:21.857 --> 00:01:22.553
Words.

00:01:22.953 --> 00:01:26.402
U V C is the USB video class, for cameras.

00:01:26.802 --> 00:01:31.213
U A C is the USB audio class, versions one, two and three.

00:01:31.613 --> 00:01:37.128
UAC / UAC2: USB Audio Class (version 2 is the modern one): the standard "I am a mic or speaker" language. Like: The same universal plug idea as the webcam language, but for sound.

00:01:37.428 --> 00:01:43.523
H I D is the human interface device class: keyboards, but also mute and hook switch buttons.

00:01:43.923 --> 00:01:50.228
HID: Human Interface Device: the standard language for buttons, keyboards and mice, also used for mute and call buttons. Like: The universal remote-control language: any computer knows what "button pressed" means.

00:01:50.528 --> 00:01:56.007
C D C A C M is a serial port over USB, great for debug consoles.

00:01:56.407 --> 00:02:00.668
And probe and commit is how a U V C host and camera agree on a format.

00:02:01.068 --> 00:02:06.769
probe(): The driver function the kernel calls when it finds a device that matches the driver. Like: The first handshake when a translator is introduced to the person they will translate for.

00:02:07.819 --> 00:02:11.232
Here's what happens when a laptop app opens the camera.

00:02:11.632 --> 00:02:16.450
The host sends a probe request with the format, frame size and frame interval it wants.

00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:21.877
On the device, the U V C function turns this into an event for the user space streaming app.

00:02:22.277 --> 00:02:29.383
User space: Where normal programs run, outside the kernel, protected from crashing the whole system. Like: The public area of a building, separate from the staff-only control room.

00:02:29.633 --> 00:02:34.381
The app fills in the probe structure, including the maximum payload per transfer.

00:02:34.781 --> 00:02:38.369
The host reads the probe back, to see what the device agreed to.

00:02:38.769 --> 00:02:39.790
Then it commits.

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:45.519
It selects an alternate setting big enough for that payload. This reserves bandwidth on the bus.

00:02:45.919 --> 00:02:48.485
The streaming app receives a stream on event.

00:02:48.885 --> 00:02:51.962
It starts queueing frames from the ISP or encoder.

00:02:52.362 --> 00:02:56.936
ISP: Image signal processor: the chip block that turns raw sensor data into a good-looking picture. Like: A photo lab that develops the raw film into a finished print.

00:02:57.236 --> 00:03:07.732
And the device sends isochronous payloads, each with a small header and then image data. If any step here is wrong, the classic symptom is: the camera opens, but the picture is black.

00:03:08.132 --> 00:03:21.053
Isochronous transfer: A USB transfer type with guaranteed timing but no retries, used for live video and audio. Like: A live TV broadcast: always on time, and a dropped frame is simply skipped, never resent.

00:03:22.153 --> 00:03:27.784
Every isochronous packet starts with a payload header. The first byte is its length, often twelve.

00:03:28.184 --> 00:03:33.246
The frame I D bit toggles at each new frame. That's how the host finds frame boundaries.

00:03:33.646 --> 00:03:36.769
The end of frame bit marks the last packet of a frame.

00:03:37.169 --> 00:03:42.719
Optional presentation time and source clock fields let the host sync audio and video.

00:03:43.119 --> 00:03:45.940
An error bit tells the host to drop a bad frame.

00:03:46.340 --> 00:03:52.273
Then the image data follows. Wrong F I D or E O F handling causes torn or merged frames.

00:03:53.473 --> 00:03:54.889
Audio and buttons.

00:03:55.289 --> 00:04:01.768
U A C one works everywhere. U A C two supports high speed and higher sample rates, with clock entities.

00:04:02.168 --> 00:04:08.414
A device with its own crystal should use asynchronous mode, with a feedback endpoint that tells the host the real rate.

00:04:08.814 --> 00:04:14.955
Endpoint: A numbered mailbox on a USB device; each data stream uses its own. Like: Numbered pigeonholes in an office: mail for video goes in one slot, audio in another.

00:04:15.205 --> 00:04:17.794
Volume and mute are controls on a feature unit.

00:04:18.194 --> 00:04:23.500
For Teams and Zoom, buttons use the H I D telephony page: hook switch and phone mute.

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:29.717
And output reports from the host drive your LEDs, so the device's mute light follows the app.

00:04:30.917 --> 00:04:32.693
Composite device rules.

00:04:33.093 --> 00:04:40.024
Group each function's interfaces with interface association descriptors, and use device class E F, two, one.

00:04:40.424 --> 00:04:43.663
Descriptor: A small table of facts the device reports about itself: who made it, what it can do. Like: A business card plus resume the device hands over when it is first plugged in.

00:04:43.913 --> 00:04:47.234
Keep interface numbers stable across firmware releases.

00:04:47.634 --> 00:04:52.847
Bump b c d Device whenever descriptors change, because Windows caches by it.

00:04:53.247 --> 00:04:56.614
And test every release on Windows, macOS and Linux.

00:04:57.814 --> 00:05:03.921
Question for you: Why does the call-control button use the human interface class rather than a custom protocol?

00:05:09.221 --> 00:05:17.789
Answer: Because every operating system and meeting app already understands that class, so the button works everywhere with no driver.

00:05:18.889 --> 00:05:19.585
Recap.

00:05:19.985 --> 00:05:22.447
Standard classes mean no driver install.

00:05:22.847 --> 00:05:27.142
For video: probe, commit, select an alternate setting, then stream.

00:05:27.542 --> 00:05:31.664
The frame I D bit toggles each frame, and end of frame closes it.

00:05:32.064 --> 00:05:34.374
Use asynchronous U A C with feedback.

00:05:34.774 --> 00:05:38.826
And H I D telephony controls give certified mute behavior.
